    <h2>Where this level actually goes wrong<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Four failure points account for nearly every stall in Train Station, and none of them is a puzzle you have to be clever to solve.</p>
    <h3>The note is not a checklist for the board</h3>
    <p>Seven times, six board rows, and the instruction says “three incorrect times”. Players who try to make the board match the note line by line start hunting for a District row that does not exist. The note is a lookup table you cross-reference, not a list to copy wholesale.</p>
    <h3>Two different pieces of paper, same name</h3>
    <p>The map fragment and the cup sheet are both labelled as a piece of paper in the inventory. One fills the metro map at 0:36, the other answers the coffee shelf at 4:00. If the cup shelf will not accept anything, check that you opened the chest properly and took the paper out of it. The map fragment is spent by then and will not do.</p>
    <h3>The wagon row starts short, and it is not obvious</h3>
    <p>Three wagons in a row, one empty slot at the end, and a locomotive with four coloured wheels. Until you take the loose wagon the game offers you at 0:16, the row cannot be completed, and dragging the three you have around will never satisfy four colours. Two swaps finish it once the set is whole.</p>
    <h3>The two black cups</h3>
    <p>On the cup shelf, the chalkboard cup and the jazz cup are both black with white artwork. Sorting them by “the black one” will get you one right and one wrong. Match the artwork: the chalkboard cup has the coffee-cup outline with the words 100% ARABICA, the jazz cup has the saxophone player in the red suit.</p>

